1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,960
Speaker 1: Hey all, Peter Zion here, coming to you from Colorado.

2
00:00:02,380 --> 00:00:05,860
Speaker 1: We are about to do the thing that we do every three months on our Patreon system,

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,440
Speaker 1: and that's you guys can send in questions and try to stump me or get something really interesting answered.

4
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:16,580
Speaker 1: You just need to sign up for Patreon at the analyst level, send in your questions, and watch me squirm.

5
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:22,440
Speaker 1: Our next question time will be the 21st of November, that's the Friday before Thanksgiving,

6
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:26,520
Speaker 1: so hope to see you there, and make sure the questions are really, really awkward.

7
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:31,020
Speaker 2: Hey all, Peter Zion here, coming to you from Ranchera Creek in Yosemite,

8
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:37,340
Speaker 2: where I have my own personal beach and pool resort, quite refreshing and a little chilly.

9
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:42,580
Speaker 2: Anyway, today we're going to talk about Myanmar, which I'm going to, of course, make some of you say,

10
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:44,860
Speaker 2: what the hell is a Myanmar, and why would I care?

11
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:45,780
Speaker 2: Well, let me tell you.

12
00:00:46,260 --> 00:00:51,480
Speaker 2: Myanmar is a country in Southeast Asia, sandwiched between the part of India that India doesn't like to talk about,

13
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:53,480
Speaker 2: and Thailand.

14
00:00:55,100 --> 00:01:00,960
Speaker 2: It is, well, it's having a really nasty civil war again, has been for decades, honestly.

15
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:07,120
Speaker 2: And we now have news that on the 28th of December, the ruling junta, a military group,

16
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:09,360
Speaker 2: has decided that they're going to hold elections again.

17
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:13,040
Speaker 2: And this is important for the three reasons that you should care.

18
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:18,300
Speaker 2: Number one, if we are going to move into a world beyond China.

19
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:20,660
Speaker 2: The Chinese are dying, so we need to.

20
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:26,840
Speaker 2: But if we still want stuff, we need to build out as robust of manufacturing supply chains outside of mainland China as we possibly can.

21
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:28,440
Speaker 2: Myanmar can be a huge part of that.

22
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:31,920
Speaker 2: It's already part of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations,

23
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:36,840
Speaker 2: but unlike Malaysia or Thailand or Singapore, it's not particularly advanced,

24
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:39,140
Speaker 2: and Vietnam long since left it in the dust.

25
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:43,960
Speaker 2: However, all types of manufacturing that are going to be economically viable

26
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:46,420
Speaker 2: require multi-step manufacturing supply chain steps,

27
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:48,560
Speaker 2: something that some policymakers sometimes forget.

28
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:51,880
Speaker 2: And that means you need someone on the low end to do things like assembly,

29
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:54,820
Speaker 2: and Myanmar is perfectly set up for that.

30
00:01:55,200 --> 00:02:00,240
Speaker 2: So if we're going to have a reasonable chance of still having manufactured goods in a post-China world,

31
00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:02,840
Speaker 2: Myanmar would really help if they were part of the solution.

32
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:03,440
Speaker 2: That's one.

33
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:08,660
Speaker 2: Number two, like I said, there's a civil war going on.

34
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:14,300
Speaker 2: And because of that, you've got lots of secondary groups, minorities,

35
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:19,060
Speaker 2: that are basically massively funding their operations through the heroin trade.

36
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:24,000
Speaker 2: Well, in the past, when the military has given up power to central control,

37
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:28,920
Speaker 2: the war has died down, and some of the heroin production and smuggling has died down with it.

38
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:31,720
Speaker 2: So yes, yes, yes, Americans love their meth,

39
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:34,920
Speaker 2: and they love their fentanyl and their cocaine, but we like heroin too.

40
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:38,940
Speaker 2: And anything that constrains the supply, I would say, is a good thing.

41
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:42,320
Speaker 2: And then the third reason why it matters is China directly.

42
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:46,300
Speaker 2: If China is ever going to escape parts of its geography,

43
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:48,300
Speaker 2: it really needs another outlet to the world.

44
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:51,300
Speaker 2: Right now, its entire population is basically on the eastern coast.

45
00:02:52,020 --> 00:02:54,980
Speaker 2: And just off the coast is something called the First Island Chain.

46
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:57,820
Speaker 2: Japan, Taiwan, the Philippines, Indonesia.

47
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:02,480
Speaker 2: Countries that are, for all practical purposes, informal, if not formal, U.S. allies.

48
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:08,000
Speaker 2: And so China has never, ever been able to punch through the chain in any meaningful way.

49
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:11,140
Speaker 2: They would take a navy as powerful as the U.S. Navy,

50
00:03:11,260 --> 00:03:14,000
Speaker 2: which they are nowhere close, to even try.

51
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:17,420
Speaker 2: So, what about an alternative on land?

52
00:03:17,420 --> 00:03:21,180
Speaker 2: Now, going through Russia sucks because it's, you know, Russia, and they can't be trusted by anyone.

53
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:26,180
Speaker 2: But Myanmar provides an opportunity to not only bypass the Strait of Malacca,

54
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:29,220
Speaker 2: but get closer to, say, the oil fields of the Middle East.

55
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:35,780
Speaker 2: So, in the past, when the military has been in control, relations with the Chinese have been pretty good,

56
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:38,640
Speaker 2: even though the Civil War boils right up to China's border.

57
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:43,920
Speaker 2: But when the military gives up power and the civilian authorities take over,

58
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:48,380
Speaker 2: then it's a little bit different, and generally China gets shafted, loses all of its investments,

59
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:50,760
Speaker 2: and Myanmar opens to the West.

60
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:54,580
Speaker 2: That's what happened about 12, 13 years ago when I was writing The Accidental Superpower.

61
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:56,820
Speaker 2: So, there's a section on Myanmar in that book.

62
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:03,840
Speaker 2: If, if, if, if, if, in December, real elections actually happen,

63
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:06,840
Speaker 2: I see no reason why that shouldn't happen again.

64
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:12,460
Speaker 2: So, having a military dictatorship in Myanmar is something that the Chinese actually like

65
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:13,340
Speaker 2: because they know who to talk to.

66
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:16,440
Speaker 2: They know that, you know, you don't have to change policy every election.

67
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:19,080
Speaker 2: Now, I don't want you to get overexcited here.

68
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:22,280
Speaker 2: Democracy in Myanmar looks different than it does anywhere else.

69
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:30,800
Speaker 2: This is a country where the Burman people are largely in the center of the country along the Irrawaddy River.

70
00:04:32,020 --> 00:04:36,600
Speaker 2: To be perfectly blunt, the military is all Burman, and they're wildly racist,

71
00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:38,500
Speaker 2: and that's one of the reasons why the Civil War is going.

72
00:04:38,860 --> 00:04:43,080
Speaker 2: And the last time we had elections, and this woman by the name of Ayan Suu Kee won,

73
00:04:43,080 --> 00:04:45,180
Speaker 2: who had been a dissident for quite some time,

74
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:48,120
Speaker 2: policy towards the minorities didn't change at all.

75
00:04:48,260 --> 00:04:51,220
Speaker 2: The Burman are pretty nasty with all of the minorities in country.

76
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:54,600
Speaker 2: So, I don't want to suggest this is a once-and-done and not part of a process,

77
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:56,780
Speaker 2: but it would definitely be a step in the right direction.

78
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:05,380
Speaker 2: One more thing to keep in mind, almost unique in Asia, certainly in Southeast Asia,

79
00:05:05,580 --> 00:05:09,540
Speaker 2: there is a river that goes right through the Burman Territory called the Irrawaddy,

80
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:16,100
Speaker 2: and it is one of only a handful of navigable waterways in the Asian landmass.

81
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:24,460
Speaker 2: So, not only would the skill set for manufacturing be kind of like a 1980s Mexico,

82
00:05:25,140 --> 00:05:29,860
Speaker 2: you know, relatively low-skilled, relatively low education, but really, really cheap,

83
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:33,160
Speaker 2: you also have the possibility, because of the navigable waterway,

84
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:35,240
Speaker 2: of moving cargo back and forth really, really cheaply.

85
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:38,600
Speaker 3: So, from an infrastructure point of view, it would be really easy to build out Myanmar

86
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:40,680
Speaker 3: if the politics align.

87
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:42,300
Speaker 3: We'll find out in December.

88
00:05:47,100 --> 00:06:17,080
Thank you.
